  <section id="hook">
    <h2><span class="secno">&sect;</span>Coding agents are optimizers, not question-formulators</h2>
    <p>
      Give a strong coding agent an objective it can measure, and it starts to look like a
      <strong>universal optimizer</strong>. It can write code, run experiments, read failures, revise
      its approach, and keep descending on the objective for as long as we can afford. It is already
      surprisingly good at the part of research that begins with: <em>make this number better</em>.
    </p>
    <p>
      What it is much less reliable at is deciding <em>which number should be better</em>. Formulating
      the right question, choosing a faithful proxy, and noticing that the objective itself is wrong
      still require judgment that optimization cannot supply. The near-term opportunity for
      auto-research is therefore not to ask agents to invent research agendas from nothing. It is to
      find problems where humans can formulate the question and agents can scale the search for an
      answer.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="thesis">
    <h2><span class="secno">&sect;</span>The thesis</h2>
    <p>
      A good auto-research problem has an <strong>NP-like shape</strong>: finding a strong candidate
      requires searching a wide space of interacting design choices, but checking a candidate is
      much easier than discovering it. This is an analogy about the shape of the workflow, not a
      formal complexity classification.
    </p>
    <p>
      That asymmetry is what makes parallel agents useful. Once part of research becomes explicit
      candidate generation followed by repeatable verification, we can spend more compute exploring
      branches. But breadth alone is not enough: the evaluator has to be cheap enough to call often,
      robust enough to trust, and aligned with the actual research objective.
    </p>
    <Callout label="Working thesis">
      <p>
        The strongest auto-research problems combine four properties:
        <strong>a wide, combinatorial design space; cheap evaluation; robust evaluation; and aligned
        evaluation.</strong> Each property is necessary, and each deserves its own design discipline.
      </p>
    </Callout>

    <div class="protos">
      <article class="proto">
        <h3>0 · Wide and combinatorial</h3>
        <p>There are many plausible branches, and the value of one choice depends on other choices.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="proto">
        <h3>1 · Cheap</h3>
        <p>Candidates can be evaluated frequently enough to support broad, iterative search.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="proto">
        <h3>2 · Robust</h3>
        <p>The score survives reruns, perturbations, and held-out cases instead of rewarding noise.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="proto">
        <h3>3 · Aligned</h3>
        <p>Higher scores correspond to research outcomes we actually care about.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="s0">
    <h2><span class="secno">0</span>Wide and combinatorial design space</h2>
    <p>
      Auto-research has leverage when progress depends on choosing among many hypotheses,
      representations, algorithms, experimental settings, and ways of combining them. The space
      should be too broad to enumerate comfortably and too interaction-heavy for one local gradient
      to settle the question. A single idea may look weak alone but become strong in combination
      with two others.
    </p>
    <p>
      This is the search side of the NP-like analogy. Parallelism buys coverage only when branches
      actually explore different regions; launching many agents that inherit the same assumptions
      merely reproduces one trajectory at greater cost.
    </p>
    <h3>Questions for this section</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>How can we tell genuine combinatorial structure from ordinary parameter tuning?</li>
      <li>How should agents partition the space without prematurely excluding strange ideas?</li>
      <li>How do we preserve useful discoveries while keeping branches behaviorally diverse?</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="s1">
    <h2><span class="secno">1</span>Cheap evaluation</h2>
    <p>
      Search scales only if feedback is inexpensive relative to generating the next candidate.
      Cheap means more than low dollar cost: evaluation must also have low wall-clock latency, low
      setup overhead, and minimal dependence on scarce human attention. The relevant quantity is the
      marginal cost of one more trustworthy comparison.
    </p>
    <p>
      Cheap evaluation creates a tight loop: propose, test, retain the useful artifact, and branch
      again. If every verdict requires a week-long experiment or an expert panel, parallel candidate
      generation only moves the bottleneck downstream.
    </p>
    <h3>Cheap does not mean compute-starved</h3>
    <p>
      Each iteration has at least two compute budgets: one for producing the candidate and one for
      verifying it. The ambition and quality of the resulting artifact probably scale with the
      first. With more compute inside an iteration, an agent can test deeper changes, run broader
      ablations, optimize implementations, and explore ideas whose value is not visible in a toy
      prototype. A tiny generation budget systematically favors ideas that are easy to demonstrate,
      not necessarily ideas that are best.
    </p>
    <p>
      Verification compute has a floor as well. If the verifier is too weak, it becomes hackable:
      shallow tests reward shortcuts, small workloads hide scaling failures, and trivial solutions
      can look competitive. The target is therefore not the cheapest possible evaluation, but the
      cheapest evaluation that remains robust and aligned under optimization pressure.
    </p>
    <Callout label="Scaling claim">
      <p>
        Scale compute <strong>inside each agent iteration</strong>, not only the number of agents,
        outer-loop iterations, or hours of human attention. Candidate-generation compute and
        verification compute should grow together.
      </p>
    </Callout>
    <h3>Questions for this section</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Can evaluation be staged, with cheap screens before expensive confirmation?</li>
      <li>What should be cached or shared across branches to lower marginal cost?</li>
      <li>When does evaluator throughput, rather than agent intelligence, become the binding limit?</li>
      <li>How does artifact quality scale with compute available inside one iteration?</li>
      <li>What minimum verification budget prevents shortcuts from dominating the search?</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="s2">
    <h2><span class="secno">2</span>Robust evaluation</h2>
    <p>
      A cheap score is useful only if it is stable enough to guide search. Robust evaluation gives
      similar judgments across reruns, seeds, environments, and reasonable perturbations. It should
      distinguish real improvements from measurement noise and brittle tricks that happen to pass
      one test.
    </p>
    <p>
      At scale, even small evaluator weaknesses become optimization targets. A broad search will
      eventually find lucky noise, leakage, and edge cases. Robustness therefore includes
      replication, held-out tests, uncertainty estimates, and adversarial checks—not merely a
      deterministic script.
    </p>
    <h3>Questions for this section</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>How many reruns are needed before a branch earns more budget?</li>
      <li>Which perturbations expose overfitting without making evaluation prohibitively expensive?</li>
      <li>How should uncertainty propagate into branch selection?</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="s3">
    <h2><span class="secno">3</span>Aligned evaluation</h2>
    <p>
      Alignment asks whether the evaluator ranks candidates by the outcome the research is actually
      trying to produce. A metric can be cheap, repeatable, and completely wrong. Once agents can
      search broadly, Goodhart&rsquo;s law stops being a warning at the margin and becomes the default
      behavior of the system.
    </p>
    <p>
      Aligned evaluation may require multiple objectives, hard constraints, hidden validation sets,
      or periodic human judgment. The goal is not a perfect scalar representation of scientific
      value. It is a selection rule whose improvements remain meaningful when optimization pressure
      becomes much stronger than it was during benchmark design.
    </p>
    <h3>Questions for this section</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>What failure would score well while violating the real objective?</li>
      <li>Which important qualities are missing because they are difficult to measure?</li>
      <li>How often should human or downstream reality checks recalibrate the evaluator?</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="architecture-note">
    <h2><span class="secno">Ex</span>Architecture research and the reoptimization valley</h2>
    <p>
      Architecture research is a useful stress test for this framework. It is hard to formulate an
      objective that directly rewards algorithmic innovation. If the objective is immediate
      end-to-end performance, a genuinely new paradigm begins at a severe disadvantage: the
      incumbent has already been co-optimized with compilers, kernels, memory systems, accelerators,
      and distributed training stacks.
    </p>
    <p>
      A large paradigm shift must first pay a <strong>reoptimization cost</strong>. The search has to
      adapt the algorithm to current hardware, find and remove immature implementation bottlenecks,
      and often redesign surrounding systems before the underlying idea can be compared fairly.
      Direct objective descent will usually reject the idea while it is still crossing this valley,
      unless the search has a strong bias or protected budget for algorithmic novelty.
    </p>
    <Callout label="Rough notes to develop">
      <ul>
        <li>
          Mature baselines carry years of hardware and software co-optimization; new algorithms are
          evaluated before receiving the same treatment.
        </li>
        <li>
          A novelty prior, protected exploration budget, or staged evaluator may be necessary to
          keep paradigm shifts alive long enough to reveal their potential.
        </li>
        <li>
          Early inefficiency may be implementation debt rather than a fundamental weakness, so a
          single end-to-end score can be prematurely hostile to innovation.
        </li>
        <li>
          The awkward possibility must remain visible: some algorithms simply do not parallelize
          well, map cleanly to hardware, or produce real speedups after fair reoptimization.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </Callout>
    <h3>The evaluator-design problem</h3>
    <p>
      The hard question is how to distinguish a temporary ecosystem tax from a structural mismatch.
      A useful architecture evaluator may need stages: first test algorithmic promise and scaling
      behavior, then estimate engineering headroom and hardware compatibility, and only later demand
      end-to-end speedup. Too little bias locks search into the current paradigm; too much bias keeps
      elegant but impractical ideas alive forever.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>What evidence justifies paying the cost to reoptimize a new architecture?</li>
      <li>Which inefficiencies are contingent on today&rsquo;s implementation or hardware?</li>
      <li>Which failures reveal fundamental limits to parallelism or achievable speedup?</li>
      <li>How should novelty and realized performance trade off as evidence accumulates?</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="s4">
    <h2><span class="secno">4</span>The conjunction matters</h2>
    <p>
      These four features multiply rather than substitute for one another. A narrow space does not
      need massive search. An expensive evaluator starves the loop. A noisy evaluator selects luck.
      A misaligned evaluator efficiently discovers the wrong answer. The promising case is the
      conjunction: difficult discovery, easy-enough verification, trustworthy comparisons, and a
      score that points toward the real objective.
    </p>
    <Callout label="Problem-selection checklist">
      <ul>
        <li>Can many meaningfully different candidates be generated and combined?</li>
        <li>Can each candidate receive feedback cheaply and quickly?</li>
        <li>Will that feedback survive replication and perturbation?</li>
        <li>Would optimizing it aggressively still move the research goal forward?</li>
      </ul>
    </Callout>
  </section>
</ReportLayout>
